## 5. Property Vocabulary

### 5.1 Lifecycle Properties (All Layers)

| Property | Type | Description |
|----------|------|-------------|
| status | enum | proposed, accepted, implemented, deprecated, removed |
| owner | string | Team or role accountable for the node |
| as-of | date | Date the assertion was last confirmed |
| confidence | number (0.0–1.0) | Certainty in the assertion (see Section 8) |

### 5.2 Specification Properties (Layer 1)

| Property | Type | Description |
|----------|------|-------------|
| priority | enum | must, should, may |
| testable | boolean | Whether the requirement has an objective pass/fail check |
| verified-by | list | TEST- / AC- node ids that establish conformance |
| stability | enum | stable, evolving, experimental |
| rationale-ref | reference | DEC- node explaining why this requirement exists |

### 5.3 Interface Properties (Layer 1)

| Property | Type | Description |
|----------|------|-------------|
| contract-version | string | Semantic version of the interface contract |
| compat | enum | stable, beta, deprecated, breaking |
| protocol | string | Transport / protocol (HTTP, gRPC, message-queue, CLI, library) |
| idempotent | boolean | Whether repeated invocation is safe |
| error-model | string | How failures are surfaced (status codes, error types, retries) |

### 5.4 Implementation Properties (Layer 2)

| Property | Type | Description |
|----------|------|-------------|
| verified-against | string | Commit ref, tag, or build id the observation describes |
| path | string | Source location (path, package, or module coordinate) |
| realizes | list | SWE.SPEC node ids this implementation fulfills |
| language | string | Implementation language / runtime |
| public-surface | boolean | Whether the node is part of a stable external surface |

### 5.5 Deviation Properties (Layer 2)

| Property | Type | Description |
|----------|------|-------------|
| deviation-type | enum | missing, extra, behavioral, performance |
| disposition | enum | accepted, bug, spec-stale, wont-fix |
| detected-by | reference | The check, test, or review that surfaced the gap |
| blast-radius | list | Nodes affected if the deviation is left unresolved |

### 5.6 Verification Properties (Layer 3)

| Property | Type | Description |
|----------|------|-------------|
| exercises | list | SWE.SPEC node ids this verification covers |
| last-run | date | When the check was last executed |
| last-result | enum | pass, fail, error, skipped, flaky |
| check-type | enum | unit, integration, property, static, acceptance |
| coverage-note | string | What the verification does and does not establish |

### 5.7 Decision Properties (Layer 4)

| Property | Type | Description |
|----------|------|-------------|
| decision-date | date | When the decision was ratified |
| alternatives | list | Options considered and rejected |
| invalidating_assumption | string | Premise whose change warrants reopening |
| supersedes | reference | Prior DEC- node this replaces |
| status | enum | proposed, accepted, superseded, reversed |

---

## 6. Domain-Specific Edge Types

### 6.1 Specification-to-Implementation Edges

| Type | Layer | Description |
|------|-------|-------------|
| swe.REALIZES | entity | Implementation node fulfills a specification node |
| swe.DIVERGES_FROM | semantic | Implementation contradicts or falls short of a specification node |
| swe.CONSTRAINS | causal | Invariant or NFR constrains a component or interface |
| swe.EXPOSES | entity | Component exposes an interface contract |
| swe.CONSUMES | entity | Component depends on another's interface contract |

### 6.2 Verification Edges

| Type | Layer | Description |
|------|-------|-------------|
| swe.VERIFIES | semantic | Test / check establishes conformance to a specification node |
| swe.COVERS | semantic | Verification exercises a component or interface |
| swe.REGRESSES | temporal | A previously passing check now fails |

### 6.3 Structural and Decision Edges

| Type | Layer | Description |
|------|-------|-------------|
| swe.DEPENDS_ON | causal | Component / module requires another at build or run time |
| swe.SUPERSEDES | temporal | Decision or contract version replaces an earlier one |
| swe.MOTIVATES | causal | Decision motivates a requirement, component, or constraint |
| swe.BREAKS | causal | Change to a contract breaks a dependent consumer |

---

## 7. Document Type Tags

### 7.1 Specification Tags

| Tag | Description |
|-----|-------------|
| SPEC | Normative specification document |
| INTERFACE | Interface / API contract document |
| SCHEMA | Data model / schema document |
| CONSTRAINT | Invariant and non-functional requirement catalog |

### 7.2 Implementation Tags

| Tag | Description |
|-----|-------------|
| IMPL | Implementation description (as-built) |
| RUNBOOK | Operational procedure for a running component |
| DEVIATION | Catalog of known gaps between spec and implementation |

### 7.3 Verification Tags

| Tag | Description |
|-----|-------------|
| TESTPLAN | Test strategy and coverage map |
| ACCEPTANCE | Acceptance criteria for a feature or release |

### 7.4 Decision and Meta Tags

| Tag | Description |
|-----|-------------|
| DECISION | Architecture / engineering decision record |
| DELTA | Semantic change record (per the versioning appendix) |
| BRIEF | Point-in-time engineering brief (may become stale) |

---

## 8. Confidence and Status Methodology

### 8.1 Confidence on Implementation Nodes

Implementation nodes describe a moving target and default their confidence from freshness:

- Confirmed against current HEAD (`verified-against` = tip): 0.95
- Confirmed against a prior tagged release: 0.80
- `as-of` older than the last release, unverified since: decays 0.05 per release cycle
- Inferred from documentation rather than read from code: cap 0.60

### 8.2 Status Transitions on Specification Nodes

A specification node moves `proposed → accepted → implemented → deprecated → removed`. The transition to `implemented` requires at least one `swe.VERIFIES` edge from a passing verification node; a linter warns when a node claims `implemented` with no passing check.

### 8.3 Deviation Aging

A `DEV-` node with `disposition: bug` that outlives N release cycles without resolution is escalated by the linter. `disposition: spec-stale` deviations are a signal to update Layer 1, not Layer 2.

---

## 9. Temporal Semantics

### 9.1 Observation Currency

Every SWE.IMPL and SWE.TEST node carries `as-of` and (for implementation) `verified-against`. A traversal engine encountering an implementation node whose `verified-against` predates the current HEAD SHOULD flag it as potentially stale.

### 9.2 Contract Versioning

Interface nodes are versioned with `contract-version`. A breaking change mints a new interface node with a `swe.SUPERSEDES` edge to the prior version; the prior node is retained with `compat: deprecated` until all consumer edges are migrated.

### 9.3 Decision Supersession

When a decision is reversed or replaced, a new DEC- node is created with a `swe.SUPERSEDES` edge and `supersedes` property. The old node's `status` becomes `superseded` and is retained for the audit trail — what was believed, and when.

---

## 10. Traversal Policy

```yaml
traversal:
  default_entry_points: ["SYS-INDEX"]
  primary_layers: [causal, temporal]
  focus_areas:
    architecture:
      entry: ["SYS-INDEX"]
      layers: [entity, causal]
      keywords: ["architecture", "system", "component", "structure", "overview"]
    interfaces:
      entry: ["API-INDEX"]
      layers: [entity, semantic]
      keywords: ["api", "interface", "contract", "endpoint", "schema"]
    requirements:
      entry: ["REQ-INDEX"]
      layers: [semantic, causal]
      keywords: ["requirement", "spec", "must", "invariant", "constraint"]
    verification:
      entry: ["TEST-INDEX"]
      layers: [semantic]
      keywords: ["test", "coverage", "verify", "check", "acceptance", "gate"]
    deviations:
      entry: ["DEV-INDEX"]
      layers: [semantic, causal]
      keywords: ["deviation", "gap", "drift", "bug", "mismatch", "todo"]
    decisions:
      entry: ["DEC-INDEX"]
      layers: [causal, temporal]
      keywords: ["decision", "why", "rationale", "trade-off", "alternative"]
    dependencies:
      entry: ["DEP-INDEX"]
      layers: [causal]
      keywords: ["depends", "dependency", "coupling", "blast radius", "impact"]
```

---

## 11. Corpus Structure

```
<system>/docs/
├── spec/                              # Layer 1
│   ├── 01-system-overview.lts         # SYS-, CMP- nodes
│   ├── 02-requirements.lts            # REQ-, NFR- nodes
│   ├── 03-interfaces.lts              # API-, IF- nodes
│   ├── 04-data-models.lts             # SCHEMA- nodes
│   └── 05-invariants.lts              # INV- nodes
├── impl/                              # Layer 2
│   ├── 10-modules.lts                 # MOD-, SVC-, FN-, EP- nodes
│   ├── 11-dependencies.lts            # DEP- nodes
│   └── 12-deviations.lts              # DEV- nodes
├── test/                              # Layer 3
│   ├── 20-test-plan.lts               # TEST-, CHK- nodes
│   └── 21-acceptance.lts              # AC-, GATE- nodes
├── decisions/                         # Layer 4
│   └── DEC-{short-slug}.lts           # One decision per file
├── events/
│   └── quality.jsonl                  # Quality event stream
└── deltas/                            # Semantic change records
    └── DELTA-{date}-{short-sha}.lts
```

---

## 12. Section Template — Component Documents

Implementation component documents (`IMPL` tag) follow a consistent template so any reader can locate the same information in the same place:

1. Overview — one paragraph, what the component is for
2. Information Scope — what this document does and does NOT cover
3. Responsibilities — the specification nodes this component realizes (`swe.REALIZES`)
4. Interfaces — contracts exposed (`swe.EXPOSES`) and consumed (`swe.CONSUMES`)
5. Dependencies — runtime and build (`swe.DEPENDS_ON`)
6. Invariants — constraints that must hold (`swe.CONSTRAINS`)
7. Known Deviations — `DEV-` nodes with disposition
8. Verification — tests and checks that cover the component (`swe.COVERS`)
9. Related Decisions — DEC- nodes that shaped it (`swe.MOTIVATES`)

